        <section id="daemon-protocol" class="docs-section">
          <h2>Daemon Protocol & IPC Multiplexing</h2>
          <p>Fastty includes an integrated, zero-overhead Unix domain socket IPC daemon (<code>~/.local/state/fastty/fasttyd.sock</code> with <code>0600</code> permissions). It enables external tools, scripts, and plugins (Neovim, Zed, VS Code) to interact with running terminal sessions without stealing window focus.</p>

          <h3>Listing Active Sessions</h3>
          <div class="code-block">
            <pre><code># List all active tabs and split panes
fastty sessions

# Watch session changes in real time (reactive stream)
fastty sessions --watch</code></pre>
          </div>

          <h3>Attaching Interactively</h3>
          <div class="code-block">
            <pre><code># Interactive attachment (raw PTY streaming, Ctrl+\ to detach)
fastty attach 1

# Read-only preview (monitor builds/servers without sending keystrokes)
fastty attach 1 --read-only

# Wait for a session to appear before attaching
fastty attach 1 --wait=30</code></pre>
          </div>

          <h3>Controlling Sessions from Scripts</h3>
          <div class="code-block">
            <pre><code># Spawn a headless session (prints its id; no command = user's shell)
id=$(fastty spawn --cwd ~/api -- bun run dev)

# Type into it (with --enter to submit), resize it, close it
fastty write $id --enter 'git status'
fastty resize $id 120 40
fastty close $id</code></pre>
          </div>

          <h3>JSON Wire Protocol</h3>
          <p>All communication uses newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON) over the Unix socket. Key commands include <code>hello</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>subscribe_sessions</code>, <code>attach</code>, <code>detach</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>resize</code>, <code>spawn</code>, <code>close</code>, and <code>binary_snapshot</code>. The full wire spec lives in the repo's <code>docs/daemon-protocol.md</code>.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Opt-in acks:</strong> <code>write</code> and <code>resize</code> accept <code>ack: true</code> and reply with a <code>done</code> event (or <code>error</code>) so scripts get truthful exit codes. Clients that omit it keep the silent streaming behavior.</li>
            <li><strong>Error codes:</strong> <code>bad_request</code>, <code>no_such_session</code>, <code>invalid_base64</code>, <code>not_attached</code>, <code>read_only</code>, <code>spawn_failed</code>, <code>not_closable</code> (GUI panes are protocol-protected).</li>
            <li><strong>Binary snapshots (FST1 v2):</strong> <code>binary_snapshot</code> returns the full terminal state — compressed scrollback + screen + cursor — restored in microseconds by the web client.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="web-gateway" class="docs-section">
          <h2>Web Gateway & Fastty-Wasm</h2>
          <p>Access your active Fastty terminal sessions from any web browser, iPad, or remote machine via the native embedded Web Gateway (<code>fastty gateway</code>) and WebAssembly client (<code>fastty-wasm</code>).</p>

          <h3>Starting the Gateway</h3>
          <div class="code-block">
            <pre><code># Start on default port 8765 (http://localhost:8765)
fastty gateway

# Bind to custom port and network interface (e.g. Tailscale / LAN)
fastty gateway --port 8765 --host 0.0.0.0

# Enforce read-only access for all browser sessions
fastty gateway --port 8765 --read-only</code></pre>
          </div>

          <h3>Pure WebAssembly VT Engine</h3>
          <p>The web client is powered by <code>crates/fastty-wasm</code>, an optimized VT emulation engine compiled to WebAssembly that renders directly into an HTML5 <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code>:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Zero External Dependencies:</strong> No heavy JavaScript terminal libraries needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Batched 2D Canvas:</strong> Batched font rendering and background quad fills at 60/120 FPS.</li>
            <li><strong>Interactive Controls:</strong> Zoom font in/out, draggable scrollbar, mouse wheel, and touch/trackpad navigation.</li>
            <li><strong>RFC 6455 WebSockets:</strong> Low-latency streaming bridge directly to the Fastty daemon socket.</li>
            <li><strong>Binary Snapshot Restore:</strong> on reconnect the client restores the full terminal state — including scrollback — from a compressed binary snapshot (FST1 v2) in microseconds, not by re-parsing ANSI.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
